{ignore}
<p><code>{null}</code> answers NULL, the level's third state next to the hit of
<a href="?tag/true">{true}</a> and the else of <a href="?tag/false">{false}</a>. In the null
state nothing of the pair renders: not the content and not its <code>&#64;else&#64;</code> part
either. Alone it prints nothing.</p>

<p>What a NULL does fire is the <code>null='name'</code> option every tag takes: the stored
<a href="?tag/content">{content}</a> of that name renders in the level's place. An
<code>else=</code> option does not fire on NULL - it is kept for FALSE, an empty list and an
empty text. A tag of your own (or a PHP call) that answers NULL for "there is no such thing"
therefore has its own way out, separate from "there is something, and it is empty".</p>

<p>The engine reads INF and NAN as NULL too. <code>{null}</code> is mainly there to show and
test that difference, and to switch a block off so thoroughly that even its else part
stays away.</p>
{/ignore}
